As shown in the figure, let points \( A, F_{1}, F_{2} \) be the left vertex and the left and right foci of the ellipse \( \frac{x^{2}}{4} + \frac{y^{2}}{3} = 1 \), respectively. From point \( A \), a line with slope \( k \) is drawn intersecting the ellipse at another point \( B \). Connect \( B \) to \( F_{2} \) and extend it to intersect the ellipse at point \( C \).
(1) Find the coordinates of point \( B \) in terms of \( k \);
(2) If \( F_{1}C \perp AB \), find the value of \( k \).

Prove that:

a) \(a = r(\cot(\beta / 2) + \cot(\gamma / 2)) = r \cos (\alpha / 2) / (\sin (\beta / 2) \sin (\gamma / 2))\);

b) \(a = r_{a}(\tan(\beta / 2) + \tan(\gamma / 2)) = r_{a} \cos (\alpha / 2) / (\cos (\beta / 2) \cos (\gamma / 2))\);

c) \(p - b = r \cot(\beta / 2) = r_{a} \tan(\gamma / 2)\);

d) \(p = r_{a} \cot(\alpha / 2)\).

Draw external normals from the vertex \( S \) of the trihedral angle \( S a b c \): \( a', b', c' \) to the faces \( b S c, c S a \) and \( a S b \) respectively. This results in a new trihedral angle \( S a' b' c' \), called the polar angle with respect to the angle \( S a b c \). Prove that: 
1. The trihedral angle, polar to \( S a' b' c' \), is the original angle \( S a b c \);
2. The sum of the measure of the plane angle \( a S b \) of the given trihedral angle and the measure of the dihedral angle at edge \( S c' \) in the polar angle is \( 180^\circ \).

Calculate the arc length of the curve given by the parametric equations:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \left\{\begin{array}{l}
x=8 \cos ^{3} t \\
y=8 \sin ^{3} t
\end{array}\right. \\
& 0 \leq t \leq \frac{\pi}{6}
\end{aligned}
$$
